from among the Tabi‘in and one of the narrators of hadith.", "newValue": "Hammām ibn Munabbih (c. 660 CE – 750 CE), a prominent Islamic scholar from Yemen, stands as a pivotal figure among the Tabi‘in, the generation succeeding the companions of the Prophet Muhammad. Born during the early Umayyad Caliphate, his life spanned a critical period in the nascent Islamic world, marked by the rapid expansion of Islamic knowledge and the urgent need to preserve the Sunnah. His enduring significance lies in his meticulous compilation of hadith, particularly the *Sahifah Hammam ibn Munabbih*, which represents one of the earliest written hadith collections and offers invaluable insight into the methods of transmission during the 1st and 2nd centuries AH.

His most notable achievement, the *Sahifah*, comprises 138 hadith narrated directly from the esteemed Companion Abu Hurayrah (d. 681 CE), who was among the most prolific narrators. Hammām ibn Munabbih diligently recorded these traditions, often dictating them to his students, ensuring their accuracy and preservation. This collection is unique for its early date and its direct lineage to a single major Companion, distinguishing it from later, more comprehensive compilations. Scholars like Imam Ahmad ibn Hanbal (d. 855 CE) and others would later incorporate these very traditions into their larger works, underscoring the *Sahifah*'s foundational role in hadith literature.

The *Sahifah Hammam ibn Munabbih* served as a critical precursor to the monumental hadith collections of the 9th century, such as those by Imam Bukhari and Imam Muslim. Its existence demonstrates the early efforts to document and systematize prophetic traditions, providing a tangible link between the oral transmission of the Companions and the later written canons. This direct, early record helped to solidify the methodology of hadith criticism and authentication, influencing generations of Islamic scholars and jurists who relied on such primary sources for understanding Islamic law and theology."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-26T08:20:21Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45781315635", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "660 CE"}, {"title": "Theological debates and doctrinal development", "type": "Idea", "year": "660 CE"}], "newValue": ["The rapid expansion of the early Islamic empire necessitated the systematic preservation of the Prophet Muhammad's teachings and practices.", "The passing of the Prophet's Companions created an urgency among the Tabi‘in to record their direct narrations before they were lost.", "Abu Hurayrah's prolific narration of hadith provided a rich source of traditions that required dedicated compilation and documentation."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-26T08:20:21Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45781315635", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Developed theological frameworks influencing doctrine", "type": "Idea", "year": "750 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "750 CE"}], "newValue": ["His *Sahifah* provided one of the earliest written collections of hadith, serving as a critical foundational text for subsequent scholars.", "His work contributed to the developm